 ## Overview
-Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) for Tomography is a metric used to evaluate the robustness of a tomographic reconstruction method against acquisition noise [S1, unofficial]. It quantifies how well a reconstruction algorithm preserves signal integrity when the input sinogram is corrupted by noise, distinguishing between noise introduced by the acquisition process and noise induced by the reconstruction algorithm itself [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard image quality metrics that compare a reconstruction to a ground truth, SNR for Tomography compares two reconstructions: one from a clean sinogram and one from a noisy sinogram [S1, unofficial]. This sets it apart from metrics like SSIM or MSE which measure fidelity to an ideal target, as SNR specifically isolates the method's sensitivity to input perturbations [S1, unofficial].
+Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) for Tomography is a diagnostic metric that evaluates the robustness of a tomographic reconstruction method against acquisition noise [S2, unofficial]. It quantifies how well a reconstruction algorithm preserves signal integrity when the input sinogram is corrupted by noise, specifically isolating sensitivity to input perturbations rather than absolute fidelity to a ground truth [S2, unofficial]. Unlike metrics such as SSIM or MSE which compare a reconstruction to an ideal target, SNR for Tomography compares two reconstructions: one derived from a clean sinogram and one derived from a noisy sinogram [S2, unofficial]. This distinguishes it from sibling metrics like [Explicit Radon Transform Analysis](9108-explicit-radon-transform-analysis.md) or [Error Volume Analysis](15301-error-volume-analysis.md), which assess structural or geometric deviations [G].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input 1:** A clean sinogram $R_\theta(\rho)$ (ideal, noise-free acquisition).
-*   **Input 2:** A noisy sinogram $R_\theta^{noise}(\rho)$ (acquired with additive noise).
-*   **Input 3:** The reconstruction algorithm $\mathcal{R}$ (e.g., Back Projection, Fourier Reconstruction).
-*   **Output:** The SNR value in decibels (dB), representing the robustness of $\mathcal{R}$.
+*   **Input 1:** A clean sinogram $R_\theta(\rho)$ representing an ideal, noise-free acquisition [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Input 2:** A noisy sinogram $R_\theta^{noise}(\rho)$ generated by applying an acquisition noise model to the clean sinogram [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Input 3:** A reconstruction algorithm $\mathcal{R}$ (e.g., Back Projection, Fourier Reconstruction) [G].
+*   **Output:** The SNR value in decibels (dB), quantifying the reconstruction's robustness [S2, unofficial].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Sinogram:** A 2D array where rows correspond to projection angles $\theta$ and columns to detector positions $\rho$.
-*   **Reconstructed Image:** A 2D or 3D array of pixel/voxel intensities.
